Date: Thu, 25 Feb 1993 12:07:24 -0500 (EST)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: POLI: In Praise of Martial Arts Training

I'm testing for my red belt (3nd Kup) in Moo Do on Saturday.  Herewith a few
thoughts on the value of martial arts training for extropians.

One of my major reasons for beginning martial arts training was my belief that
every libertarian has a moral obligation to learn effective self-defense, up
to and including the use of lethal force.

Why?  First, because it's humbug to say that you'll fight to the death to
defend your liberty if you can't *fight*.  Yes, yes, I know all about contract
security associations and PPL and all those civilized methods for socializing
the costs of defensive violence.  Come Libertaria, I'll use them too.  But
what happens when the criminal is breaking in *your* door?  And a lot of us
fantasize about smashing the State --- that's bullshit unless you're able and
willing to stop the State's minions dead.

Second, I insist that it's important --- indeed, a vital part of ethical
maturity for any libertarian --- to know that the power of life and death,
the responsibility for the ultimate ethical decision --- rests with *you*.
That you can kill, if necessary, with full mindfulness and taking full
responsibility for the execution and the result.  And, even more importantly,
to know that, having the ability to do effective lethal violence, *you can
refrain from it*.

Until you *know*, in your gut, what you would do when faced with the threat
of lethal aggression, all your intellectual choices about violence will be
sham, piffle, purest bullshit.

Does this mean that I think all libertarians have to turn into Rambo,
grunting and waving hardware at each other to prove their authenticity?  No.
That's certainly not what I've chosen.

There are two issues here.  One is learning truly effective self-defense.  For
that, the right thing is to buy a handgun and learn to maintain and use it.
Ideally, you should take the kind of "combat shooting" course police academies
offer --- training in fast reaction to a mix of targets, some hostile, some
friendly.  I plan to do this, but haven't yet.

The other issue is developing what I think of as "warrior mind" --- a gut-
level understanding of the uses and consequences of violence, the courage
to face it, and the serenity to refrain from it.  Firearms training isn't
so good for this, because even when the intent of firing a gun is defensive
the effect is pure offense.  Also, and more importantly, the gun
psychologically disconnects you from the violence.  You don't feel your
bullet striking.

This is where martial arts comes in.  It's much less likely to save your
life in a practical way, but it's much better for cultivating warrior mind.
In martial arts, you feel the shock of contact in your arms, your legs,
your torso.  Violence is personal, real, immediate.  You learn to make use-
of-force decisions quickly and correctly even while under physical stress.

Most importantly, you learn a kind of calm, and confidence, and centeredness,
that is extremely valuable for *avoiding* violence.  Only the man (or woman)
with warrior mind can be truly peaceful --- the untrained, under pressure, are
too likely to snap into either panic flight or ill-directed and ill-considered
violence.

Nor is this path limited to the young and athletic.  I'm 35 and have congenital
cerebral palsy.  Though there are some physical techniques closed to me, the
cultivation of "warrior mind" has already proved immensely valuable for me.

There are other satisfactions as well, especially for women and children and
unathletic intellectual males culturally conditioned to feel physically
incompetent.  The first time you shatter an inchand-a-half-thick pine board
with one bare hand will live forever in your memory...trust me on this. :-)

Then, too, there are the obvious health benefits.  I hate exercise, especially
calisthenics.  But there's no denying that it extends lifespan and lowers one's
risk of heart disease and numerous degenerative ills.  Martial arts is valuable
not least because it supplies a motivating context for regular exercise, a way
of getting it that's not boring and that has instrumental benefits beyond just
the exercise.

For all these reasons, I strongly recommend martial arts training to all
extropians.  I study Moo Do, which is a modern Tae Kwon Do (Korean karate)
with a lot of stuff imported from Okinawan karate and Aikido.  But style
is much less important than quality of instruction --- all styles merge
together at the higher levels.  Find a good school and do it!
-- 
							>>eric>>

