Date: Thu, 25 Feb 1993 09:36:34 -0800 (PST)
From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: LAW: custom vs first principles

On Wed, 24 Feb 1993 drw@bourbaki.mit.edu wrote:

> The Yanamamo seem to be one of they very few "primitive" cultures that
> were reached by the anthropologists before they were pacified by
> colonists.  The colonists, coming from market-based societies, see one
> of their principle purposes the suppression of warfare, as warfare
> hurts trade.  Thus, most cultures studied by anthropologists have had
> their internal warfare suppressed by a colonists for a generation or
> more before they were studied.  The Yanamamo (at the time Chagnon
> studied them) were still engaging in continuous skirmish warfare,
> although there is some indication that their internal warfare has been
> intensified by the effects of indirect contact with industrial
> culture.
> 
> Interestingly, the Yanamamo are also a "pre-market" type of culture;
> there are very few quid-pro-quo transactions.  There are a lot of
> cases where resources are distributed based on kinship, and
> gift-exchange relationships are common, and some cases where favors
> are returned, but there are no explicitly quid-pro-quo trades, as far
> as I can tell.
> 
> What little I know of the New Guinea highlanders matches this
> description also.
> 
> It's likely that before the development of the first "states" (about
> 4000 BC in Mesopotamia), all of human culture was like this.
                           ^^^ ^^ ^^^^^ ^^^^^^^ ^^^ ^^^^ ^^^^
Like which?  Pre-market, undoubtedly.  Like the Yanomamo in terms
of the level of warfare and internecine violence, certainly not.
In the first place, this sort of perpetual skirmish warfare probably
always involves settled, usually agricultural, communities, which
didn't exist until about 10,000 years ago, and weren't widespread
until considerably later than that.  It also, probably for the
fairly obvious ecological reasons, turns up mostly where there's
some degree of population pressure, which is an even more recent
development in most of the world.  Typically, nomadic immediate-forager
hunter-gatherer cultures--which means all human cultures up until
10,000 years ago, and most for a long time thereafter--show a
very low incidence of both inter- and intra-group violence.
     And, the Yanomamo are notorious for the level of violence
in their culture.  They have a few rivals around (the Jivaro are
one, I think), but very few cultures are attested that show
anything remotely like that level of intra-community violence--
and there's enough data on pre-colonial situations to show that
this isn't simply a post-colonial development.

