Dani Eder:
> if there are any subpopulations 
> carrying memes for high birth-rate, then they will eventually
> dominate the population.  

This is only occurs where the memetic replication is tightly bundled
with the genetic replication.  Such subcultures actively filter 
the mass media, public schools, etc., eg the Hutterian Brethren 
(who have no TVs or radios, tightly control the phones, and keep 
schooling in the colony), and to a lesser extent the Mormons (who 
have a quite unique pro-natalist theology in addition to their less 
effective attempts at media filtering).  

> If this growth rate differential persisted for a century, then
> the couple of million Mormons would grow to six times as many.

This is only true if the Mormons can keep their children in the
fold.  The Hutterites lose over half their children to "the
world", so their phenomenal birth rate (8 per lifetime) is more
than cut in half when following their population through the
generations.  I suspect Mormons also lose a high proportion these
days, it would be interesting to see statistics.  Furthermore, 
except for their emerged capability of boundless expansion, these 
memes are quite un-extropian and I do not find their population of 
the universe to be a satisfactory outcome.

> Of course, we extropians expect events to render such questions
> moot before then, don't we?

I think extropians differ widely on our timelines for longevity,
and even more widely on our timelines for uploading, copying, etc.
My own guess is that mean lifespan in developed countries will 
increase only slowly during next 100 years, while the mean fertility
span will not rise significantly, and fertility rates will continue
to fall.  A few lucky, skilled, and extropian-motivated folks will
start greatly exceeding the mean with techniques like cryonics, 
antioxidants, neural transplant, gene therapy, cell repair, etc.  
Some may also extend fertility span with expensive high-tech means.
But the numbers will be too small to effect the mean, and may not
be sustainable memetically.  

As of _today_, having and raising children is infinitely more effective 
than uploading or copying in fulfilling boundless expansion and 
implementing a form of evolving immortality.  Furthermore, there is 
synergy as well as tradeoffs between having children and extending life.
So, why not do it?  In most cases it doesn't makes sense to use longevity 
as a reason to have fewer children.

