Date: Thu, 25 Feb 93 17:11:27 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: EXT: Selective breeding

   From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)

   Dale Worley:
   > Yes, well-educated women have few
   > children, but that's probably because that's the optimal number of
   > children for them to have (see The Selfish Gene). 

   _The Selfish Gene_ does not argue this; in fact quite the opposite: 
   genetic fitness is measured by the number of genes propagated to
   the next generation.  It is unlikely that in any developed
   country well-educated women benefit their sibs' fertility to
   any significant extent.  The effect would have to be quite dramatic 
   to counterbalance their lack of genetic fitness vs. high school dropouts, 
   which is almost a 1:2 ratio.   Their sibs would have to have 4 times
   as many children as the sibs of high school dropouts for their genetic
   fitness to catch up!

No, the optimum is based on the question "What number of children
should I have to maximize my contribution to the future generations?"
(modulo a few details)  The answer to this question might well be
below the replacement level, if that was indeed the optimum.  There is
no reason to assume that the maximum contribution for one group is the
same as the maximum contribution for another.

The interesting and subtle questions come when one attempts to figure
out the sociobiological forces involved.  Clearly, in the
college-educated class of Americans, a significant effect is the high
expense of raising children, since college has to be paid for.
Probably, this is a major factor in limiting the number of children.
But this only applies to legitimate children -- males in the class
have the option of having illegitimate children.  It may be that the
bulk of the future contribution of a female in the class is via
illegitimate children of her sons than via legitimate grandchildren.

Or it may must be that the life-strategy of the college-educated class
is suboptimal.  It appears that human brain size has not increased in
the last 100,000 years.  Perhaps there is a *reason* for this.

Etc.  There are a million ways to figure this.  What I'd like to see
is some real statistics showing the breeding patterns of Americans, so
one could start predicting what would be the optimum strategies for
individuals in various situations.

Dale

