From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

Dani Eder writes:
>I've been thinking of a case where it would be rational to take
>a high risk investment, even if the expected return is negative.

There must be many cases where a person's utility is not a linear function
of money.  If you owe the Mafia $1000 and only have $20, it may be rational
to put in on a horse at 50-1 which has only a 100-1 chance of winning,
since the difference between $20 and zero is trifling compared with not
getting a concrete overcoat.

There are people to whom a huge sum of money is worth little more than
enough to live simply on, but enough to live simply on is worth a lot more
than nothing at all. (I doubt if there are many on this list!)

Money, unlike utility, has the useful property of being easily measurable. 
One should not mistake it for utility on the grounds of mathematical
convenience.

