>    Sounds like the Norse legal code. The mere fact that its private doesn't
>    mean it is sanctioned. In other words, what you are describing is a
>    private legal system -- not a system in which murder carries no
>    sanction. I believe you've just demonstrated our point for us.
> 
> Hmmm...  Well, then, what *is* your point?  If you describe murder as
> being "sanctioned" in a society where murderers are punished if (and
> only if) someone else objects to the murder (a happenstance that is by
> no means universal), I would say that "sanctioned" does not capture
> the essence of what I call "law" -- namely the existence of a set of
> *rules* (implicit or explicit) as to which behaviors are not
> permitted, and *mechanisms* to actively suppress and punish
> non-permitted behavior.

Such mechanisms are ALWAYS dependant on someone objecting. If someone shoots
you and no one cared to do anything about it, they'd get away with it no
matter what some dusty books in the library said. Law is always dependant
on custom -- you can't avoid that, period. Human law is not like physical
law.

PPL, customary law, or whatever you call it, embraces this notion. Torts
are the only kind of offense that exists, and they are handled solely
by action of the victim or the victim's proxies. If you don't want
to call this a legal system, thats fine, but we are using that as our
accepted local terminology, and will continue to do so, I suspect.

> Or to cut through the verbiage, I can't imagine *any* state of society
> which does not match your definition of "private legal system" (as I
> understand the term from your paragraph above).

Our current society doesn't match it -- there is a notion of offenses
against the state, and there is no competition in jurisprudence or enforcement
services.

